A||||26||frontal lobe||hard drive||||1||
Z||000000||frontal lobe||05-08-2000||10:37 PM||||Can anyone help me, please?  My computer runs windows 98 and has a 5gig hard drive.  Well, I keep getting messages that my hard drive is filled.  With what I have loaded on it, it shouldn't be close.  When I look under my computer, it says I have a 2gig hard drive.  What gives?  Is there some kind of automatic hard drive petitioning default?  If there is, how do I reset it?  TIA.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||05-08-2000||11:13 PM||||What kind of computer do you have? Processor speed? It sounds like your BIOS may not support the large drive, but it may just be an incorrect setting in the bios. Most BIOS chipsets have an "auto-detect" for the hard drives. If the wrong parameters are assigned to your drive, it will not show full capicity. You could try to manually enter the specifications for your drive and see if that fixes it. Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||05-08-2000||11:15 PM||||you can change the partition on the harddrive by using Fdisk in dos, but that requires you to delete all your info.<P>Try going to the start button, clicking programs then accessories, then system tools, then finally click system information.<BR>A window will pop up and show what you have on your C drive exp. 500meg out of 1000meg.<P>if its 2000meg out of 2000 meg, its possible your partition is messed up.  <BR>if its like 5000 out of 5000, try using cleanup in system tools.<P>But reply on what sys info says on your harddrive condition.||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||05-08-2000||11:19 PM||||Is your windows 98 an upgrade from 95?  If so you will have 2 gig partitions.||||reg||1
Z||000004||frontal lobe||05-08-2000||11:34 PM||||Cooter, you know how it is with fiero guys.  We like to do things ourselves.  So I got an old case from a friend for free, got a motherboard off e-bay from some guy for 35 bucks with on-board sound and video (it was new, but what manufacturer?  Who knows??), bought a 233mmx (not PII), 32mb ram, 36x cd-rom and mouse and keyboard free after rebate, 5 gig maxtor hard-drive, and voila!  I'm up and running for under $250 bucks. Unbelievably, it actually works fine.<P>wkayl, BINGO!  I didn't want to steal windows from someone (can't steal) so I got windows '95 (for free, also) from someone who wasn't using that machine anymore.  Then I got the 98 upgrade and loaded it on top of it.  So that's why I'm stuck with a 2 gig partition!  Any idea what to do next?  I hate wasting the rest of the hard drive.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-08-2000||11:47 PM||||My suggestion is back-up your HD. Create a start-up disk from your win98 CD. Then boot off of disk (with CD-ROM support) go into Fdisk and remove all partions. Set it up for 1 partion at 5 gig (It may already be there<BR>and show 5gig) Then Format hard drive with the win 98 startup disk. You'll get all 5G + it will format in "FAT32" which will be a lot better too!. Then go to win98 CD and run setup. It will give you a message that it cannot find qualifying software on the HardDrive and it will ask for a location. Put the win95 CD into the drive. It will read it and then it will ask for the win98 CD. You do not need to load win95 first! This will then load win98 ONLY onto your HD! (you can also do this with win 3.1 disks 1,2,4,& 6) I have setup many computers for friends and this is the easiest way to do it. Win95 release A limits you to 2 gig win 95 release B or win98 you are good up to 22gig.<P>PLEASE NOTE THIS WILL WIPE THE HARDDRIVE CLEAN. IF YOU HAVE FILES YOU CANNOT REPLACE YOU WILL LOOSE THEM.||||reg||1
Z||000006||frontal lobe||05-08-2000||11:59 PM||||Thanks, Oreif!  I thought I did that on initial set-up.  I know right now the files are FAT 32.  I don't mind so much clearing the hard drive because I really don't have anything on it I care about losing.  Just really the pain of re-loading the software.<P>What do people with windows 95 do with their hard-drives when it uses 2gig partitions.  Any easier way around this?||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||05-09-2000||12:29 AM||||Whoa whoa.... Don't get too crazy just yet.  If you're seeing one drive in the "my computer icon" and it's 5gigs total, you've only got one partition. If not, you can run fdisk and select option 4 (view partition status) and see how you're setup.  Tell me how many partitions are listed, and what types. IE: primary dos, non-dos, and the type of file system (fat16 fat32)||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||05-09-2000||11:27 PM||||Doc, what Oreif is telling you is correct if you want to convert to a one partition drive. BUT, use Windows Explorer to check to see if you have a D: and maybe an E: drive that is not a CD-Rom drive. If you do have a D:, but that is the CD-Rom, then you can still create another partition on your harddrive using Fdisk without losing what's already on it. If C: is partitioned to 2 megs, then you can create a D: for the remaining 3 megs of space on the harddrive. The CD-Rom will automatically be assigned E: drive.||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||05-11-2000||01:30 AM||||OK.  Thanks a lot guys.  You are dealing with a novice here, so speak s l o w l y and clearly and in small words!  So I got into fdisk on dos and found that it reads me as having a 6.1gig hard drive (Is supposed to 5gig-go figure).  Then for some reason (which I am likely unwittingly responsible for) I got one partition of 2gig size formatted in fat16!<P>I had 4 options.  I didn't know what to do with them.  I would for now rather just add another partition as another drive if possible.  Wouldn't that be easiest?  I would like to wait on re-formatting the hard drive into one partition until I assemble another computer in a couple of months and load windows 98 on that one and just do both at the same time.  Currently the D drive is the cd-rom.  What do I do next???||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-11-2000||11:08 AM||||IF this mother board fully supports drives over 2GB. This is a Pentium class mother board so it's posible that it doesn't support &gt;2GB drive right. <P>Get a copy of Partition Magic. Follow the directions that come with it. Partion Magic is a graphical version of FDSIK that can stretch partitions and convert them to FAT32 at the same time without reformating and reinstalling things. <P>If it's an Intel VX chipset or older that has a new drive Then you may have issues either with drives over 2GB or drives over 8GB. Some "clone" chip sets from SIS and others also have problems with drives over 2GB.<P>The 2 GB issues are:<BR>1. the computer won't talk to the drive at all. Not a problem here. (This can be BIOS or a chipset problem.)<BR>2. the computer will talk to the dive but cannot properly handle boot partitions over 2 GB. (This is a BIOS problem.)(My Acer at work does this. It will talk to my 3+GB drive, but the boot partition must be below 2 point something GB or BIOS & Win95 have a cow.)<P>The 8GB limit can be either a BIOS or chipset problem. Some Intel chipsets don't talk to drives over 8GB no matter what the BIOS is. (My machine at home has this problem.)<P>Many machines come/came with fat16 2GB boot partitions on large drives becuase Microsoft didn't/doesn't really support FAT32 in Win95. I don't know if official support for it in Win98 exists or not. Microsoft originally provided FAT32 with no support at all. Not even for big makers like Compaq, HP, or IBM could get support for it so they refused to ship machines with it or support it if you installed it yourself.<P>FAT32 also causes problems for some hard disk replicators used in manufacturing the computers. (Compaq and the rest are not about to replace very expensive machines just to provide FAT32.) This is likely the main reason that even now many machines come with FAT16 boot partitions.<P>Note: All Win98 and WinNT 4.x CD's are bootable if the system BIOS suports that. You may have to go into "BIOS setup" and turn it on.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||frontal lobe||05-12-2000||01:25 AM||||Ogre, thanks for the info.  I am pretty sure the chipset is a sis one.  As I mentioned, I just got a cheap motherboard to throw something together as a first attempt at this.  It probably only handles one 2gig partition as in fdisk that is the only option it gives me as far as I can tell.  I don't even know who made the motherboard.  Oh well.  Guess I'll have to get another motherboard.  Again, to all, thanks for all the good advice. I appreciate it.<P>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||05-12-2000||09:08 PM||||If you're running Windows 98, there is a utility that can change FAT16 to FAT32 without losing anything. BTW, yes you can add another partition and that would be the easiest for now.<P>Be very careful with Fdisk, you could easily erase your whole drive with the wrong keystroke. Get back into Fdisk again. On the first screen if it says do you want to enable large format size, say yes, this is FAT32. At the menu where you have about 4 choices, enter 1, create DOS partition or Logical DOS drive. At the next menu, enter 2, create Extended DOS partition. It should now tell you how much is left on the drive that is available to make a partition. Just use the maximum allowed and follow the instructions. If it only allows 2 meg partitions, you may have enough for a third partition too.||||reg||1
Z||000013||frontal lobe||05-13-2000||02:41 AM||||Batboy,<P>Thanks for all the tech help!  The good news:  I got into fdisk, did what you said, and didn't destroy anything!  It recognized I have 4.1gig left (again, why a "5 gig hard drive has 6.1, I don't know.)  It entered it automatically as the partition size and I accepted.  Then it asked if I wanted logical dos.  I don't know what that means, but I accepted.  Then it said I would have to restart the machine AND REFORMAT THE HARD DRIVE.  More good news.  I went to windows explorer and now I have a 2 gig c-drive and the cd-rom is my e-drive.  The bad news:  my new d-drive doesn't say anything about having 4.1gig available.  Also, the c-drive says ms-dos 6 by it but the d-drive doesn't have anything by it.<P>Did I do something wrong?  How do I reformat this new partition?  If I get it going, how do I use it?  When I go to load cd's, will it ask which "drive" to use?  I made it this far, but where do I go from here?  P.S.  I accepted large format size, but I still don't know if I'm in FAT32 yet.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||05-13-2000||10:39 AM||||Doc, all is well. I didn't give you every detail because I wanted you to take it step by step and not to overload your brain. I'm sure it's filled with lots of important medical stuff, not stupid computer info that will be useless in another two years anyway.<P>Congrats, you have partitioned your harddrive. Now you have to format D: drive. <B>CAUTION!</B> Do not format C: drive or you will loss all data.<P>The easiest way is to double click the My Computer icon on your desktop. In the next window, single click on D: drive. Make sure the D: is highlighted and not C: or anything else. Now click on file at the top of the window which should pop up a menu. Halfway down is format, click on that. Some message will pop up saying this is a non-removeable harddrive and are you sure you want to continue. Double check that the top of the window indeed says formating D: drive (or words to effect). If so, click the full formating box and click ok. It'll give you a message afterwards letting you know if the formatting was successful. Congrats again, you just formatted your D: drive.<P>Now to answer your other questions: How do you use this D: drive? Just like C: drive. When you load new software, it'll give you a default location it wants to load to which is always C:, just type in D: instead.<P>Windows Explorer is another way to access your drives. In fact, go to Windows Explorer now (click start button, then programs) and single click on D: drive. Go to the files pull-down menu and click on properties. This will show how much unused space the drive has left and will show if it's FAT32 or FAT16.<P>If you what to convert to FAT32, click on the start button (lower left corner of desktop), then programs, then accessories, then system tools, then drive converter. Follow the instructions and congrats, you've converted to FAT32.<P>The CD-ROM is automatically noted into the Windows registry that it was assigned a drive letter, in your case, E: drive. The only problem you might have is with previously loaded software, if you are required to leave the CD in to run the program (like many graphic intensive games) it might look for the CD-Rom drive on D: still and give you an error message. There is a way to change that, but that's another future lesson, or you can just uninstall the software and reinstall, then it'll work fine. Hope this helps. If it does, get plenty of rest, drink lots of fluids, and mail me my consulting fee check in the morning...hehehe just joking. Glad to help out!<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroSTETZ||05-14-2000||04:09 AM||||get a copy of partition magic from someone- if you are not comfy with fdisk its one of the easiest ways to got. also, what brand of drive is it? if it is a maxtor you should be able to adjust the partition size without losing data (honest! i am a technician at compusa and a maxtor rep gave us an industrial version of maxblast!) if you want email me the make and model number for the drive..||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||05-14-2000||04:47 PM||||Hope he didn't crash his harddrive or something. Doc, ya still with us? Did it work?||||reg||1
Z||000017||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||05:06 PM||||So did he create an extended dos partition?  Even if you convert to fat32 within windows, you will NOT have all 5 gigs available.  It will make your 2 gig partition a fat32, but that's all.  If you don't want to wipe out your drive, run fdisk and create the extended dos partition like batboy suggested, then you MUST choose to create a logical dos drive in that extended partition.  You need to do this before it's usable. Then you can format the drive, which will be d:<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||GT Bastard||05-14-2000||05:08 PM||||by the way... win95 CAN use fat32 given that it's 95B or later. ||||reg||1
